      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          No, dish soap does not belong in a pressure washer's detergent tank or siphon tube: it foams far more than the machine is built for, it is thick enough to starve or clog the injector, it leaves a film that attracts dirt, and most manufacturers say to use only detergents labeled for pressure washers, so using anything else can put a warranty claim in doubt. The exception is outside the machine, where a little dish soap in a bucket, applied with a brush and rinsed off with the washer, is perfectly reasonable for small jobs. Everything below covers why the tank is the problem, what it does to cars, siding and plants, the one safe way to use it, and which purpose-made detergent to buy for each job instead.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Isn't Dish Soap Recommended in a Pressure Washer?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dish soap is designed for a sink. It is concentrated, thick, and engineered to produce lots of stable foam because people associate suds with cleaning power when they wash dishes by hand. A pressure washer's chemical system is designed for the opposite: a thin, low-foaming liquid that gets drawn through a small orifice, mixed with a fast stream of water, and sprayed onto a surface where it can be rinsed away cleanly. Put the first product into the second system and almost every design assumption breaks.
          </p>

          <p>
            The most common symptom is simply that it does not work. A typical consumer washer pulls detergent through a downstream injector or an onboard tank using a venturi, which relies on low pressure at the soap nozzle to suck liquid in. Undiluted dish soap is too viscous for that small passage, so the machine draws little or nothing, and the owner assumes the soap system is broken. When people thin it enough to flow, they usually overshoot the other way and get a wand full of foam and a driveway that will not stop bubbling.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second problem is the manuals. Most pressure washer manufacturers tell you to use only detergents labeled for pressure washer use, and several warn that other chemicals can damage the pump, seals or injector. That does not mean one squirt of dish soap will destroy a machine, and we are not going to pretend it will. It does mean that if the soap system or pump fails while you have been running household products through it, you are arguing with the warranty terms rather than relying on them.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third problem is the result. Dish soap is very good at lifting grease, but it is not formulated to rinse off a vertical surface quickly under high-volume water, and a heavy dose leaves a thin film on paint, siding and glass. That film is slightly tacky and attracts dust, which is the opposite of what you wanted. On a car it also strips wax and sealant, which is why every detailing guide tells you to avoid it.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the honest answer is a split one. Inside the machine, no: buy a pressure washer detergent made for the surface you are cleaning, which costs about the same per job. Outside the machine, in a bucket with a brush, a small amount of dish soap is fine for light work on non-painted surfaces like patio furniture, plastic toys, grill exteriors and trash cans, as long as you rinse thoroughly and keep the runoff off your plants and out of the storm drain.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="machine" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Does Dish Soap Actually Do to the Machine?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the injector, because that is where most of the trouble happens. Downstream injectors and onboard soap tanks meter chemical through a tiny hole or check valve, often with a small mesh filter on the pickup tube. Dish soap is many times thicker than a pressure washer detergent, so it struggles to pass, and the residue it leaves behind dries into a gummy plug when the machine sits. The next time you use the soap nozzle, nothing comes out, and the fix is soaking and clearing a part that was working perfectly before.
          </p>

          <p>
            Foam is the second issue. Most consumer machines inject chemical downstream of the pump, so the soap never passes through the pump itself, which is why the damage is usually limited to the injector, hose and wand. But some machines and some setups, such as siphoning into the inlet or using an upstream proportioner, do send chemical through the pump. Heavy foam inside a pump introduces air, and air in a pump causes the same pulsing, cavitation and seal wear you get from a starved water supply. That is a real risk, not a theoretical one, on any machine where chemical enters before the pump.
          </p>

          <p>
            Seals and O-rings are the third consideration. Dish soaps are not especially harsh, but some contain degreasing solvents, fragrances and additives that are not what the elastomers in a pump or injector were chosen to tolerate. A single use is unlikely to cause visible harm. Repeated use, and especially soap left sitting in the system for weeks, is the pattern that manufacturers are trying to prevent when they say to use approved detergents and to flush the system after every session.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">If it foams in a sink, it does not go in the tank.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Pressure washer detergents are low-foaming by design. Anything built to make lots of suds, including dish soap, laundry detergent and hand soap, belongs in a bucket, not in the injector or onboard tank.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Finally, there is the question of who pays when something does fail. Warranty language varies by brand, but the common thread is that damage caused by improper chemicals is excluded. Running a twelve-dollar jug of purpose-made detergent through a machine that cost a few hundred dollars is cheap insurance. If you want to see which machines have soap systems worth using in the first place, our <a href="/best-electric-pressure-washer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best electric pressure washers</a> guide notes which tanks and injectors actually draw well.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="surface" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Is Dish Soap Bad for Cars, Siding or Plants?
          </h2>

          <p>
            On cars, yes, it is the wrong product. Dish soap is a degreaser, and the protective layer on a car's clear coat, whether wax, a polymer sealant or a ceramic coating topper, is exactly the kind of oily film it is designed to remove. One wash will not ruin the paint, but it leaves the finish unprotected, which means dirt bonds more easily and the next wash is harder. A car shampoo or pressure washer car wash detergent cleans just as well while leaving that protection in place.
          </p>

          <p>
            On vinyl siding, painted wood and brick, the issue is residue rather than damage. A bucket-strength solution rinsed off promptly is generally harmless, but a concentrated dose that is allowed to dry in the sun leaves a dull, streaky film that holds onto pollen and road dust. House wash detergents are formulated with surfactants that release quickly under a rinse, and many include ingredients aimed at mildew and the green algae that grows on north-facing walls, which dish soap does nothing about.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plants and lawns are the part people forget. Soap solutions can damage leaves, which is why even insecticidal soaps come with dilution limits and warnings about sensitive species. A pressure washer sprays a lot of water quickly, and everything you put into that water runs off onto the beds below the wall you are cleaning. Wet the plants with plain water before you start, rinse them again afterward, and do not let soapy runoff pool around roots.
          </p>

          <p>
            Storm drains are the last concern. In most US communities, storm drains run directly to creeks, rivers or lakes without treatment, and local rules often prohibit washing soapy water into them. Biodegradable does not mean harmless to fish and aquatic insects in concentrated form. Direct runoff onto the lawn or gravel where it can soak in, use the minimum amount of any soap, and check whether your town has specific rules for driveway and car washing.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="difference" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Makes Pressure Washer Detergent Different?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The biggest difference is foam control. Pressure washer detergents are formulated to cling briefly and then rinse away, with low or controlled foaming so that the injector draws consistently and the rinse does not turn into a twenty-minute battle with suds. Products sold as foam cannon soaps are the exception, and they are designed for a foam cannon attachment that mixes air deliberately, not for the onboard tank.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second difference is viscosity and concentration. Downstream injectors dilute whatever they draw, typically somewhere around one part chemical to ten or more parts water depending on the machine and nozzle. Detergents sold for that use are thin enough to flow through the injector and concentrated enough to still do something after that dilution. Many are sold as concentrates that you pre-mix into the tank at a ratio printed on the label, which is worth following exactly.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Property</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Dish soap</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Pressure washer detergent</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Foaming</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High, stable suds</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low or controlled</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Thickness</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thick, slow to flow</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thin, flows through injector</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Rinse behavior</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Can leave a film</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Formulated to rinse clean</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Surface-specific</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Car, house, deck, concrete versions</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Manufacturer approval</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually not</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, if labeled for pressure washers</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cost per job</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Pennies</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Roughly 1 to 3 dollars</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The third difference is that detergents are matched to the surface. Car wash formulas are pH-balanced and wax-safe. House wash formulas target mildew, algae and general grime on siding. Concrete and driveway formulas are stronger degreasers for oil spots and tire marks. Deck cleaners are usually oxygen-based and gentle on wood fibers. Dish soap is a single general degreaser, which is why it does a mediocre job on most of those surfaces and a damaging one on some.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cost is not really an argument for dish soap either. A gallon of pressure washer concentrate typically costs roughly ten to twenty-five dollars and covers several jobs, so the price per wash is a couple of dollars at most. The savings from using dish soap are trivial compared with the cost of a clogged injector or a car that needs re-waxing. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-use-detergent-in-a-pressure-washer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how to use detergent in a pressure washer</a> covers dilution, dwell time and the nozzle you need for the injector to draw at all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safe-way" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is There Ever a Safe Way to Use Dish Soap?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, and it is the old-fashioned way: in a bucket, not in the machine. Mix a small squirt of dish soap into a couple of gallons of warm water, apply it with a soft brush, sponge or a pump sprayer, let it sit for a few minutes without drying, then rinse with the pressure washer using a wide tip. The washer is only acting as a rinse hose, so no soap ever passes through the injector, hose or wand, and none of the warranty or clogging issues apply.
          </p>

          <p>
            This works well for the jobs dish soap is actually good at: greasy grill exteriors, plastic outdoor furniture, kids' toys, garbage cans, cooler lids, garden tools and resin planters. These are small, non-painted items where a bit of film does not matter and scrubbing is part of the job anyway. It is a poor choice for a whole house, a car, a deck about to be stained, or anything with a wax or sealer you want to keep.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How strong should a bucket mix be?</h3>

          <p>
            Weaker than you think. A tablespoon or two per gallon is plenty for general cleaning, and more just means more rinsing. If the surface is still greasy after one pass, a second application at the same strength works better than doubling the soap. Keep the mix out of direct sun on hot days so it does not dry on the surface, and rinse from the top down so clean water is always running over areas you have already finished.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you insist on putting dish soap in the machine anyway, which we do not recommend, reduce the damage. Dilute it heavily first so it is thin and watery, use only the low-pressure soap nozzle so the injector actually draws, never siphon it into the pump inlet, and run clean water through the soap system for a couple of minutes afterward. Expect more foam than you want and check the manual first, because some manufacturers explicitly prohibit it.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is one more case where people reach for dish soap: a foam cannon. Foam cannons attach to the wand and mix air into the solution deliberately, so foaming is the point. Even so, a foam cannon still sends soap onto the car, and dish soap still strips wax. A dedicated foam cannon car soap is the better choice, and it costs about the same per wash as a squirt of dish soap once you account for how little you need.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="instead" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Should You Use Instead for Each Job?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Match the product to the surface, and check that the label says it is safe for pressure washer use. That one phrase tells you the formula is thin enough to inject, low-foaming enough to rinse, and something the machine's manufacturer is unlikely to object to. Most big brands of pressure washers sell their own detergents, but any reputable product labeled for pressure washers should work in any standard injector.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Job</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Use this</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Avoid</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Car, truck, boat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Car wash or foam cannon soap</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dish soap, household cleaners</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Vinyl or painted siding</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">House wash detergent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Undiluted bleach, dish soap</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wood deck</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Oxygen-based deck cleaner</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Chlorine bleach, high pressure</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Concrete and driveways</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Concrete degreaser</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Muriatic acid in the machine</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Patio furniture, toys</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Bucket of mild soap, then rinse</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Running soap through the injector</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For siding, a house wash formula is the right call, and if you have mildew, look for one made for that job rather than reaching for household bleach. We cover the rules on bleach separately in <a href="/can-you-use-bleach-in-a-pressure-washer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">can you use bleach in a pressure washer</a>, because the answer there is more complicated and the stakes for the pump and the plants are higher. For vinyl specifically, our vinyl siding guide covers the low-pressure technique that avoids forcing water behind the panels.
          </p>

          <p>
            For decks, use an oxygen-based cleaner, usually sodium percarbonate, applied with a pump sprayer and scrubbed with a stiff brush, then rinse at low pressure. The <a href="/how-to-clean-a-deck/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how to clean a deck</a> guide walks through dwell times and pressure limits. For concrete, a dedicated degreaser handles oil spots far better than dish soap ever will, and a surface cleaner attachment does the rinsing evenly without stripes.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the small stuff, honestly, dish soap in a bucket is fine, and that is where it belongs. The lesson is not that dish soap is dangerous. It is that the soap system on a pressure washer was built for a specific type of chemical, and the purpose-made products are inexpensive, widely available and better at the job. When the right detergent costs a couple of dollars per wash, there is no real reason to improvise.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="already-did" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Already Ran Dish Soap Through It? Here's What to Do
          </h2>

          <p>
            Do not panic. In most cases nothing has been damaged, and a thorough flush fixes the problem. Empty whatever is left in the onboard tank or the siphon bottle, rinse the container, then fill it with clean water. Fit the soap nozzle, run the machine, and let it draw plain water through the injector for two or three minutes, or until no foam at all comes out of the wand. That clears the passages before the residue dries.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the injector has stopped drawing, the dish soap has probably gummed it or the pickup filter. Remove the filter screen on the end of the siphon tube and rinse it in warm water. Some injectors have a small check ball that sticks when residue dries; soaking the injector or tank outlet in warm water and gently working the ball free usually brings it back. If the machine uses a detachable soap bottle, soak the cap and pickup tube too.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the machine started pulsing, surging or losing pressure while you were using dish soap, stop and look at how the soap was getting in. Pulsing almost always points to air in the pump, and foam drawn into the inlet is one way to put it there. Disconnect the soap, run the machine on clean water with a full supply from the hose, and let it purge air for a minute with the trigger held open. If the pulsing continues on clean water, the cause is something else, such as a clogged inlet screen or a restricted supply.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then rinse the surface you cleaned. Any dish soap residue left on siding, glass or a car will dry into a film, and a second rinse with plain water now saves you a streaky finish later. If it was a car, plan to reapply a spray wax or sealant, since the dish soap almost certainly removed some of what was there. And next time, put the bucket method or a labeled detergent to work instead.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pouring Dish Soap Straight Into the Tank</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Undiluted dish soap is too thick for most injectors and onboard tanks. It either refuses to draw, which looks like a broken soap system, or it dries into a gummy plug that blocks the injector next time. Use a detergent labeled for pressure washers, or keep the dish soap in a bucket.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Washing the Car With It</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dish soap is a degreaser, and the wax, sealant or coating on a car's clear coat is exactly what it removes. The paint is left bare, so dirt bonds faster and the next wash is harder. A pH-balanced car wash soap cleans just as well without stripping that protection.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting Soap Dry on the Surface</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Any soap that dries on siding, glass or paint in hot sun leaves a streaky film that attracts dust. Work in shade or in sections, keep the surface wet, and rinse from the top down. If you see spots after it dries, rinse again rather than adding more soap.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Plants Below</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Soapy runoff from a wall lands directly on the beds underneath it. Wet plants with clean water before you start, rinse them again when you finish, and avoid letting suds pool around roots. A quick drink before and after prevents most of the scorched leaves people blame on the washer.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Flush</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Whatever chemical you use, leaving it in the injector, hose and wand lets it dry and stick. Run clean water through the soap system for a minute or two at the end of every session. It is the single cheapest habit for keeping a soap system working for years.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use Dawn in a pressure washer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not in the detergent tank or siphon tube. Dawn is a concentrated, high-foaming degreaser, so it either clogs the injector or floods the wand with suds, and it strips wax from cars. A small amount in a bucket, scrubbed on and rinsed off with the washer, is fine for grills, plastic furniture and trash cans.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will dish soap damage my pressure washer pump?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On most consumer machines the detergent is injected downstream of the pump, so the soap never reaches it and a single use is unlikely to cause harm. The risk rises if you siphon soap into the inlet, where foam brings air into the pump and causes pulsing and seal wear, or if soap is regularly left to dry in the system.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does using dish soap void the warranty?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can. Most manufacturers tell you to use only detergents labeled for pressure washers, and warranty terms commonly exclude damage caused by improper chemicals. Whether that applies depends on the brand and on what failed, but running household products through the machine gives a manufacturer an easy reason to decline a claim.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What can I use if I have no pressure washer soap?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a quick job, plain water at the right nozzle and distance does more than most people expect. If you need soap, mix a little mild dish soap in a bucket, scrub it on by hand and rinse with the washer. For anything larger, such as siding, a car or a driveway, buying the right detergent is worth the trip.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is laundry detergent any better than dish soap?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Laundry detergent foams heavily, often contains enzymes, brighteners and fragrances, and powders can leave grit that does not dissolve fully in an injector. It has the same problems as dish soap and some extra ones. Keep it for the washing machine and use a labeled pressure washer detergent instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why won't my pressure washer pull soap at all?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most injectors only draw when the low-pressure soap nozzle is fitted, because they rely on a pressure drop to pull chemical in. Check that you are using the black or soap nozzle, that the pickup filter is clear, and that the soap is thin enough to flow. Thick products like dish soap are a very common cause.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is homemade pressure washer soap a good idea?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. Homemade recipes based on dish soap, vinegar or bleach run into the same problems: too much foam, residue, possible damage to seals, and chemicals that can react with each other. Bleach and ammonia-based cleaners must never be mixed. Purpose-made detergents are inexpensive and labeled for the job, which homemade mixes are not.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

