        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Dish soap is not a disaster in a pressure washer, but it is the wrong tool. It is too thick and too foamy for most injectors, it leaves a film that attracts dirt, it strips wax from cars, and most manufacturers specifically tell you to use detergents labeled for pressure washers. If something fails after you have been using it, the warranty may not help.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Keep dish soap in a bucket for small, greasy, non-painted jobs, and rinse with the washer. For everything else, buy the detergent made for the surface, use the soap nozzle so the injector actually draws, rinse plants before and after, and flush the system with clean water when you are done.
          </p>
        </div>

